.TH FSCR 1M "Root Computers Proprietry"
.SH NAME
fscr - file system compaction routine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fscr
-sn:m -ttempdir -v -adays -x -r -bblocks -ddensity -llength filesystem temp
.PP
.B fscr512
-sn:m -ttempdir -v -adays -x -r -bblocks -ddensity -llength filesystem temp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I fscr \^
takes the (unmounted) filesystem specified and reorders the blocks of the
files in the following manner:-
.RS 10
inodes
.PP
directories
.PP
current executables
.PP
current regular files
.PP
free blocks
.PP
aged regular files
.PP
aged executables
.RE
The definition of current is dependant on the number of days specified with the
.B -a \^
flag. If the
.B -a \^
flag is omitted a default ageing period of 7 days is assumed.
If no ageing is required,
.B -a0 \^
should be specified.
.PP
Executable files are stored in contiguous blocks on the disk to allow for fast
loading for execution. If the
.B -x \^
flag is given the executable files will be
interleaved instead of being contiguous.

Regular files are interleaved according to the specified separation factor.

The freelist is rebuilt using the specified separation factor.

Using the user supplied definitions of number of blocks, or of density and length
an estimate of the number of volumes will be supplied before output commences.
The user will need to confirm that an adequate number of volumes are available.

The meanings of the available options are:-
.TP 10
.B a{days}
definition of current in number of days
.TP 10
.B x
interleave executable files
.TP 10
.B -s{n:m}
modify cylinder size (n) amd stepsize (m) to these values.
.TP 10
.B -r
retain directory order. The default is for directory
entries to be sorted into time of last access sequence.
.TP 10
.B -v
verbose. Print statistical information as to success
of the compaction process.
.TP 10
.B -t{tempdir}
use tempdir as the directory for the temporary files
instead of /tmp.
(Note: this option must be used if /tmp is on the filesystem
being modified)
.TP 10
.B -b
the number of 1k blocks that the media can contain. This is used for
exchangeable disks. This entry causes any -l and -d flags to be ignored.
.TP 10
.B -l
the length (in feet) of the magnetic media to be used. Default is 2300 feet.
.TP 10
.B -d
the density of the magnetic media to be used. Default is 1600 bits per inch.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SH FILES
.SH SEE ALSO
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SH WARNINGS
The lost+found directory needs to be remade after fscr has been run as empty
entries are removed from directories.
The file system must be clean (i.e. free from fsck errors) before fscr is run.
If this is not the case, results are unpredictable.

.SH BUGS
